<body>
<div class="viz-root">
<main>
<button class="toggle theme-switch" id="theme" hidden>Toggle dark mode</button>
<h1>Crater detector report</h1>
<p class="lede"><code>src/data_tools/estimate_dem_craters.py</code> finds the craters already present in a LuNaSynth base DEM and
compares their size distribution with the crater model used to add smaller craters.</p>
<nav class="toc">
  <a href="#usage">Usage</a><a href="#method">Method</a><a href="#results">Results</a>
  <a href="#validation">Validation</a><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a><a href="#references">References</a>
</nav>
<section id="usage">
<h2>Usage</h2>
<pre><code>uv run python src/data_tools/estimate_dem_craters.py &lt;base_dem.tif&gt; \
    [--age 3.9] [--terrain highland|mare] [--min-diameter-px 10] \
    [--min-depth-ratio 0.04] [--output-dir tmp/dem_craters]</code></pre>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Default</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>dem</code></td><td>&ndash;</td><td>Base DEM GeoTIFF, in meters.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--age</code></td><td>3.9</td><td>Surface age (Gyr) of the model curve. Polar highlands are about 3.5&ndash;3.9 Gyr old.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--terrain</code></td><td><code>highland</code></td><td>Degradation rate of the model curve, <code>highland</code> or <code>mare</code>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--min-diameter-px</code></td><td>10</td><td>Smallest crater to detect, in pixels. Use 4 for Shape-from-Shading DEMs, which resolve smaller craters.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--min-depth-ratio</code></td><td>0.04</td><td>Smallest depth / diameter of a crater (the visibility threshold of the model).</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>--output-dir</code></td><td><code>tmp/dem_craters</code></td><td>Folder for the CSV and the figure.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Examples</h3>
<pre><code># LOLA tile (PGDA 78): reliable craters start at ~10 pixels = 50 m
uv run python src/data_tools/estimate_dem_craters.py \
    assets/base_meshes/NPC_final_adj_5mpp_surf_piece_2km_2_1.tif

# Shape-from-Shading tile (PGDA 104): resolves craters down to ~4 pixels = 20 m
uv run python src/data_tools/estimate_dem_craters.py \
    assets/base_meshes/A310_SfS_5mpp_Leibnitz_Beta_piece_1km_r1600_c1200.tif --min-diameter-px 4</code></pre>

<h3>Output</h3>
<p>For a DEM <code>&lt;name&gt;.tif</code> the script writes two files and prints a table:</p>
<ul>
<li><code>&lt;name&gt;_craters.csv</code>: one row per crater with <code>column_px, row_px, diameter_m, depth_m</code>.</li>
<li><code>&lt;name&gt;_csfd.png</code>: cumulative size-frequency distribution of the detections with the model, the production function, and geometric saturation.</li>
<li>Printed: detected and model densities at several diameters, and the diffusivity <code>kappa_800</code> for which the model has as many craters as detected.</li>
</ul>
<pre><code>$ uv run python src/data_tools/estimate_dem_craters.py assets/base_meshes/NPC_final_adj_5mpp_surf_piece_2km_2_1.tif
{{ example }}</code></pre>
<p>The suggested <code>kappa_800</code> can be set in <code>terrain.crater_field</code> of a dataset configuration so the craters LuNaSynth adds
continue the distribution of the base DEM. It is an upper bound of the diffusivity, because the detector misses part of the craters.</p>
</section>

<section id="method">
<h2>Method</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Candidates.</strong> Scale-space maxima of the scale-normalized Laplacian of Gaussian of the elevation (Lindeberg, 1998) are bowl-shaped
depressions. The scale of the maximum gives the radius, calibrated on synthetic craters (diameter &asymp; 5.2 &sigma;).</li>
<li><strong>Regional slope.</strong> A plane fitted between 1.5 and 2.5 radii is subtracted.</li>
<li><strong>Size refinement.</strong> The rim crest of the azimuthally averaged profile refines the radius.</li>
<li><strong>Depth.</strong> As in Zhang et al. (2026): rim height is the mean elevation of a thin ring at the rim, floor is the lowest point near the center.
Candidates with depth / diameter below <code>--min-depth-ratio</code> are dropped.</li>
<li><strong>Shape tests.</strong> The topography within 1.5 radii must be nearly axisymmetric (its radial profile explains at least 60% of its variance),
which rejects most depressions of rough terrain. The surroundings must not be curved by more than half the crater depth, which rejects the walls
of craters larger than the searched range.</li>
<li><strong>Duplicates.</strong> The same depression found at several scales is kept once, at its strongest response.</li>
<li><strong>Model comparison.</strong> The model curve counts craters of the LuNaSynth model (Neukum et al., 2001 production and chronology,
degradation by the diffusivities of Zhang et al., 2026) still deeper than the visibility threshold.</li>
</ol>
</section>

<section id="results">
<h2>Results on the base DEMs</h2>
<p class="muted">All three tiles are 5 m/px polar DEMs. Detected densities are compared with the highland model on a 3.9 Gyr surface at the
smallest detected diameter. Each tile shows the detections on its hillshade (numbered), their size distribution, and a close-up of every
detection with its rim circle and azimuthally averaged profile (rim marked by the vertical line).</p>
<div class="card">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Tile</th><th>Size (km)</th><th>Smallest crater</th><th class="num">Detected</th><th class="num">Detected / km²</th>
<th class="num">Recall-corrected / km²</th><th class="num">Model / km²</th><th class="num">Matching kappa_800 (m²/Myr)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{{ summary_rows }}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
{{ tiles }}
<div class="callout">
<strong>Reading the results.</strong> On the LOLA tiles, most pits in the hillshade are interpolation artifacts between altimetry tracks; the shape
tests leave only a few real craters. The 2 km LOLA tile has more craters than the highland model, which suggests slower degradation at this polar
site than at the equatorial highland sites of Zhang et al. (2026). On the Shape-from-Shading tile, the detected craters agree with the model within
a factor of two once corrected for the detector's recall on that terrain ({{ sfs_recall }} of {{ sfs_injected }} injected craters found).
</div>
</section>

<section id="validation">
<h2>Validation</h2>
<p>Synthetic craters (fresh and degraded normal craters of 12&ndash;40 pixels, 20 per 1.5 &times; 1.5 km tile, 4 tiles) were added to rough
terrain with a power-law spectrum. A detection matches a crater if its center is within 0.3 diameters.</p>
<div class="card">
<h3>Recall on synthetic craters</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Background relief (std)</th><th class="num">Visible craters</th><th class="num">Found</th>
<th>Diameter ratio, median (10&ndash;90%)</th><th class="num">False / km²</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{{ synthetic_rows }}
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>False detections on terrain without craters</h3>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Background relief (std)</th><th class="num">False detections / km²</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
{{ null_rows }}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="muted">Diameter ratio is detected diameter over the current rim diameter of the synthetic crater. Craters overlapping a neighbor are the
most common misses. On real terrain the recall is lower than on synthetic terrain: on the Shape-from-Shading tile, {{ sfs_recall }} of craters added to
it are found.</p>
</section>
<section id="limitations">
<h2>Limitations</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Small counts.</strong> A 1&ndash;2 km tile has tens of craters at most; Poisson uncertainty on the density is ~20&ndash;40%. Fitting a size distribution
with few craters is better done with Poisson timing analysis (Michael et al., 2016), as in <a href="https://github.com/ggmichael/craterstats">craterstats</a>.</li>
<li><strong>LOLA polar DEMs.</strong> Most pixels of PGDA product 78 are interpolated between altimetry tracks, so the effective resolution is coarser than 5 m and
varies across a tile; detections there are a lower bound.</li>
<li><strong>Missed craters.</strong> Overlapping, very degraded, or irregular craters are usually missed; secondary craters are not distinguished from primaries.</li>
<li><strong>Not a catalog.</strong> For catalog-quality detection on images, see deep-learning detectors such as Silburt et al. (2019) for DEMs and
Zhuo et al. (2025) for LROC NAC images.</li>
</ul>
</section>

